#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# stirling_s2

#% text
# `stirling_s2(n, k)` gives the Stirling number of the second kind, the number of ways to split `n` elements into `k` non-empty blocks.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 26.8.1; `stirling_s2(n, n)` is 1 and `stirling_s2(n, 0)` is 0 for `n > 0`.

#% item
# `stirling_s2` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
stirling_s2(5, 2)
#% output
#= 15

#% cell
stirling_s2(5, 5)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
stirling_s2(4, range(4))
#% output
#= [1, 7, 6, 1]

#% cell
stirling_s2(30, 10)
#% output
#= 173373343599189364594756

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The number of ways to split a set of 6 into blocks, the Bell number:

#% cell
sum(stirling_s2(6, range(0, 6)))
#% output
#= 203

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The explicit formula:

#% cell
[stirling_s2(7, 3), sum((-1)^j*binomial(3, j)*(3 - j)^7, [j, 0, 3])/factorial(3)]
#% output
#= [301, 301]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `stirling_s1`, `binomial`, `partitions_p`
